From bdf0911017c2e3e9eca0a5f9853395e48a30b29d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <assistant@example.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 04:01:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix and extend visualize_rl.py: normalization bug, headless
 render, 3 verification modes

train/visualize_rl.py had gone stale relative to the rest of the
pipeline and, as written, could not be used to trust-check a trained
checkpoint at all. Fixes and additions, in the order they matter:

1. FIX (correctness): build_inference_fn built the policy network with
   the default identity preprocess_observations_fn. train_mjx.py trains
   with ppo.train(normalize_observations=True, ...), which Brax resolves
   internally to preprocess_observations_fn=running_statistics.normalize
   (confirmed in brax/training/agents/ppo/train.py). Replaying a
   checkpoint through an unnormalized network feeds it observations far
   outside its training distribution -- the robot would look broken in
   any replay regardless of how good the trained policy actually is.
   Now passes running_statistics.normalize explicitly, matching training.

2. FIX (portability): render_video (--mode video) called mujoco.Renderer,
   which defaults to GLFW (a real window) and unconditionally crashes
   with 'an OpenGL platform library has not been loaded' on any host
   without a display (headless WSL2, SSH-only boxes, CI). sys.argv is
   checked for '--mode video' before mujoco is imported and MUJOCO_GL is
   set to egl (headless GPU rendering) only in that case, leaving
   --mode interactive's real GLFW window untouched. Verified working via
   a direct EGL render smoke test.

3. ADD --disturb_step/--disturb_velocity: a one-off horizontal velocity
   impulse applied to the torso between env.step() calls, independent of
   RobotConfig.DISTURBANCE_CURRICULUM/RANDOM_PUSH_MAX_FORCE (both
   currently False/0.0, intentionally, per Phase 0 -- see robot/config.py
   comments). No run to date, including disturbed_eval_log.json, has ever
   had a real external push applied; this lets push-recovery be checked
   visually without touching that switch.

4. ADD --mode plot: runs the identical rollout (checkpoint, normalized
   inference, optional push) with zero rendering -- no GPU/display/GL of
   any kind -- and saves a 4-panel matplotlib PNG (torso height,
   tilt-from-upright in degrees, horizontal drift, per-step
   both_feet_contact) with a red line at the push step. Complements
   --mode video: subtle jitter/drift/asymmetric recovery are easier to
   misjudge from a rendered animation than to read off a time-series
   plot. Requires matplotlib (not in requirements-lock.txt yet).

5. FIX (interactive mode was a no-op): mujoco.viewer.launch_passive does
   not step physics itself -- this script always drove physics
   externally via MJX, using 'data' purely as a render mirror. Ctrl-drag
   perturbation in the viewer only ever wrote into data.xfrc_applied,
   which nothing read back before the next step_fn call, so grabbing and
   shoving the robot with the mouse had zero effect on the simulated
   trajectory -- purely cosmetic. apply_mouse_perturbation() now calls
   mujoco.mjv_applyPerturbForce(model, data, viewer.perturb) each frame
   (signature and xfrc_applied shape (nbody, 6) confirmed against the
   installed mujoco build) and folds the resolved drag force into
   state.pipeline_state.qvel before the next step, so dragging the robot
   in the live viewer now actually perturbs the MJX rollout.

Also adds scratch/simulation_output/ to .gitignore (rendered
GIFs/PNGs are run artifacts, not source).

Verification: (1)-(4) verified end-to-end (checkpoint load -> normalized
inference -> EGL render / matplotlib plot -> push injection -> file
written) against a freshly-initialized dummy checkpoint built via the
exact network/observation-size construction path train_mjx.py itself
uses; GIF and PNG output inspected directly. (5)'s API calls were
verified individually against the installed mujoco build and the actual
model (TORSO_BODY_ID=1 confirmed as the free-joint root body), but the
interactive mouse-drag path itself requires a real display this sandbox
does not have and so could not be exercised end-to-end here -- please
verify by hand.

tests/test_training_wrapper.py, tests/test_training_eval_contract.py
unaffected (this commit only touches train/visualize_rl.py and
.gitignore).
